## Ping trigger pattern for scripts/term-watch.sh (POSIX ERE, matched with grep -E).
##
## The FIRST line below that does not start with '##' (and is not blank) is used
## as the regex. term-watch.sh matches it against each (ANSI-stripped) line shown
## in `term`. When a line matches, the watcher sends `ping` to the agent session.
##
## Because matching is done on the pane's visible output, patterns should be
## anchored to a prompt so ordinary command output does not trigger false pings.
## Lines starting with '##' are comments; blank lines are ignored (so the actual
## pattern may begin with a single '#').
##
## --- Default: a comment typed at ANY prompt. A prompt ends in a SYMBOL (a
##     non-alphanumeric, non-space char: '$', '>', '%', ']', or a powerline
##     glyph), so we match '#' when it follows such a symbol + space, or starts
##     the line. This is prompt-agnostic (plain AND themed/powerline prompts) yet
##     ignores mid-output hashes, whose '#' follows a WORD (e.g. "items # ...").
## example (matches):     erik@host:~/proj$ # run the tests
## example (matches):      main  # deploy    (powerline prompt: glyph + space + #)
## example (matches):     # deploy to staging
## example (no match):    ls -la
## example (no match):    total 0 items # trailing hash in program output
## example (no match):    echo "pass#123"   (no space before '#')
## example (matches):     >>> # debug this   (Python stdlib REPL)
## example (matches):     In [3]: # summarize   (IPython — # may follow : with no space)
## example (matches):        ...: # continued   (IPython multiline)
## example (no match):    code()  # inline   (output text — filtered in term-watch.sh)
(^|[^[:alnum:][:space:]][[:space:]]+)#([[:space:]]|$)|^In \[[0-9]+\]:[[:space:]]*#|^[[:space:]]*\.\.\.:[[:space:]]*#
##
## --- Stricter: only a comment right after a classic prompt char ($, #, >, ]).
##     Use this if your prompt ends in one of those and you want the old behavior.
## example (matches):     erik@host:~/proj$ # run the tests
## (^|[]$#>][[:space:]]+)#
##
## --- Example for an IPython-based term (In [n]: prompt followed by a #comment):
## example (matches):     In [3]: # summarize the failures
## example (no match):    In [4]: x = 1
## ^In \[[0-9]+\]:[[:space:]]*#
##
## --- Example: trigger only on a double-hash marker.
## example (matches):     erik@host:~/proj$ ## ask the agent
## example (no match):    erik@host:~/proj$ # ordinary comment
## (^|[[:space:]])##
